; vim.grid - vim opening a three-line file in the alternate screen.
; Audited against corpus/vim.bin. Checked in detail: the bright-blue (SGR 94, palette 12) '~'
; filler rows, which vim draws as a tilde followed by 119 literal spaces - so each fills the row to
; column 120 and the next one lands on a wrapped continuation, which is why r04..r24 are marked
; wrapped; and the bold+inverse ruler and message lines at the bottom.
; No divergent path is exercised.
;
; Ported to the unified seam. The header key that lists wrapped rows is now 'continues', and
; ITerminalGrid.ContinuesPreviousRow(N) is true for the row that continues the one above it -
; the direction the engine already stores, and the only direction that is answerable for the
; bottom viewport row. The rows listed are exactly the ones this file has always listed, so the
; audited wrap points are unchanged; the key was renamed so that a file written under the
; earlier 'wrapped' key cannot be mistaken for one written under this one.
;
; Corrected 2026-08-28: the cursor lines here read 'steady' throughout, which was the engine
; reporting a TerminalOptions field nothing ever wrote (gap 11) and not audited terminal
; behaviour. The power-on cursor style is a blinking block - DECSCUSR Ps=0 and Ps=1 both name it
; as the default - and this is the one corpus that says otherwise: vim sends ESC [ ? 12 h at byte
; 282 and ESC [ ? 12 l at byte 288, so blinking is turned back off at byte 294. Frame 1 is taken
; at byte 87, before either, so it reads 'blink'; frames 2 to 9 are taken at byte 617 or later and
; stay 'steady'. There is no DECSCUSR anywhere in corpus/vim.bin, so the shape stays block.
;
# vim  frame 1/9  after 87/4325 bytes  (alt screen enter)
size 120x30
alt on
cursor col=0 row=0 visible shape=block blink
scrollback 0
title "C:\\Program Files\\Git\\usr\\bin\\vim.exe"
icon "C:\\Program Files\\Git\\usr\\bin\\vim.exe"
modes autowrap focus mouse=off/x10 kitty=none
continues none
text
  r00-r29 ~blank~
style
  s00-s29 ~default~
legend
    .  default

